 SwiftUI app using bearer tokens, native Apple Sign-In, and web ↔ native handoff.\n+- [Account deletion](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/account-deletion) — Cascade-delete a user across every plugin's tables.\n+- [Adding 2FA](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/adding-2fa) — Enroll, confirm, and challenge with TOTP — the full UX.\n+- [Adding a password to an OAuth or OTP account](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/adding-password) — Let an authenticated user opt in to passwords without creating a new account.\n+- [Building an admin tool](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/admin-tool) — Mount @authfn/admin behind your corporate IAM and ship a UI for support.\n+- [Canonical-gateway multi-region](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/canonical-gateway-multi-region) — One stable AuthFn authority, canonical identity placement, signed cell forwarding, migrations, and an operator runbook.\n+- [CLI authentication](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/cli-auth) — Issue an API key from your web UI and use it from a command-line tool.\n+- [Adding a custom OAuth provider (Microsoft Entra)](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/custom-oauth-provider) — Wire authfn to a provider that's not in the bundled set — Microsoft Entra here, but the pattern works for anything.\n+- [Custom validation hooks](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/custom-validation) — Use beforeUserCreate / beforeSessionIssue to enforce organization-specific rules.\n+- [Email verification](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/email-verification) — Verify a user's email after sign-up. Copy-paste recipe.\n+- [Google One Tap](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/google-one-tap) — Hand a Google Identity Services credential to authfn for a frictionless web sign-in.\n+- [Recipes](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes) — Copy-pasteable solutions for common authfn flows. Each recipe is small, self-contained, and links to the relevant plugins / SDKs.\n+- [Magic link sign-in](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/magic-link) — Send a clickable sign-in link instead of asking the user to type a code. Same kernel; different UI.\n+- [Multi-region deployment](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/multi-region-deployment) — Two regions, a globally-replicated lookup store, and a client that routes by email.\n+- [Native mobile handoff](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/native-mobile-handoff) — Sign in via a system browser, hand the session off to your iOS / Android app as a bearer credential.\n+- [OpenTelemetry instrumentation](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/observability-otel) — Wire authfn's observability events into OpenTelemetry spans for full distributed tracing.\n+- [Password reset](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/password-reset) — Full password-reset flow — start, verify, change. Two routes, three UI screens.\n+- [Placement-bound auth context](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/placement-bound-auth-context) — Trusted in-process and private-service AuthFn context for regional application data planes.\n+- [Rotating 2FA encryption keys](https://authfn.com/docs/recipes/rotate-2fa-encryption) — What the shipped two-factor plugin encrypts, and how to change keys without a private crypto API.\n+- [Changelog](https://authfn.com/docs/reference/changelog) — Release history of authfn and @authfn/* packages.\n+- [Configuration](https://authfn.com/docs/reference/configuration) — Every option you can pass to authfn() and app.createServer().\n+- [Envelopes](https://authfn.com/docs/reference/envelopes) — The shape of every authfn HTTP response — success and error.\n+- [Error codes](https://authfn.com/docs/reference/errors) — Every AuthFnErrorCode the kernel emits — HTTP status, retryability, when it fires.\n+- [Observability events](https://authfn.com/docs/reference/events) — Every AuthFnEventType the kernel emits — when it fires and what's in its metadata.\n+- [Reference](https://authfn.com/docs/reference) — The exhaustive reference — every route, error, event, table, envelope, and configuration option.\n+- [HTTP routes](https://authfn.com/docs/reference/routes) — Every authfn route, organized by plugin. Auto-generated from the OpenAPI spec.\n+- [Database schema](https://authfn.com/docs/reference/schema) — Every table generated by `@superfunctions/cli generate-schema` — fields, types, and relationships.\n+- [@authfn/client](https://authfn.com/docs/sdk/client) — The TypeScript client — works in browsers, Node, Bun, Deno; cookie or bearer; with an automatic CSRF, region-aware routing, and a typed surface for every kernel route.\n+- [authfn](https://authfn.com/docs/sdk/core) — The Node server kernel — declare an app with authfn(), then inject runtime dependencies with createServer().\n+- [SDKs](https://authfn.com/docs/sdk) — First-party authfn SDKs — Node kernel, TypeScript client, Svelte bindings, Python kernel, Swift client.\n+- [Python SDK](https://authfn.com/docs/sdk/python) — The authfn Python kernel — same routes, same envelopes, same OpenAPI as the TypeScript kernel, on FastAPI / Flask / Starlette.\n+- [@authfn/svelte](https://authfn.com/docs/sdk/svelte) — Svelte stores, context, and SvelteKit-friendly patterns for displaying sign-in state and reacting to session changes.\n+- [Swift SDK (AuthFnSwift)](https://authfn.com/docs/sdk/swift) — The Swift package for iOS / macOS — bearer-token sessions, native Sign in with Apple, web→native handoff, and SwiftUI helpers.\n \n ## API Reference\n \n-- [Authfn](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/api/authfn)\n+- [Authfn](https://authfn.com/docs/api/authfn)\n--- historical-plan/authfn/docs/static/llms-full.txt\n+++ native-generated212/authfn/docs/static/llms-full.txt\n@@ -1234,7 +1234,7 @@\n For one-off conversations, paste this into a Claude prompt:\n \n ```text\n-Reference docs: https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/llms-full.txt\n+Reference docs: https://authfn.com/docs/llms-full.txt\n \n Now help me…\n ```\n@@ -1273,7 +1273,7 @@\n - The schema is generated by `npx @superfunctions/cli generate-schema --config ./superfunctions.config.mjs --adapter drizzle --dialect postgres --output ./apps/api/src/db/generated --force` and lives in `apps/api/src/db/generated/authfn-schema.ts`.\n \n Authoritative docs: https://authfn.superfunctions.dev\n-For a full-text RAG file: https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/llms-full.txt\n+For a full-text RAG file: https://authfn.com/docs/llms-full.txt\n \n If you have access to MCP tools (`authfn.docs.search`, `authfn.openapi.operation`, `authfn.skills.invoke`), prefer those.\n ```\n@@ -1363,7 +1363,7 @@\n description: authfn\n ---\n \n-When working with @authfn/*, fetch context from https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/llms-full.txt as needed.\n+When working with @authfn/*, fetch context from https://authfn.com/docs/llms-full.txt as needed.\n ```\n \n ## Project-specific tweaks\n@@ -1445,7 +1445,7 @@\n The fastest way to get going is to point your assistant at the canonical URL:\n \n ```\n-https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/llms-full.txt\n+https://authfn.com/docs/llms-full.txt\n ```\n \n Most assistants will accept that as a context source. If your tool is MCP-aware, see [MCP](./mcp) for a richer experience that surfaces type definitions, route metadata, and example apps on demand.\n@@ -1460,94 +1460,51 @@\n \n # llms.txt\n \n-[`llms.txt`](https://llmstxt.org/) is a community convention for static, LLM-friendly summaries of a project. authfn ships **two** files at the docs origin:\n-\n-- `https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/llms.txt` — short index. Lists every doc page with a one-line description and a link.\n-- `https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/llms-full.txt` — full text. Concatenated markdown of every doc page plus the OpenAPI spec.\n-\n-Use whichever fits your assistant's context budget.\n-\n-## How they're generated\n-\n-Both files are emitted by docsfn at build time:\n-\n-```bash\n-npm run docs:llms     # writes static/llms.txt and static/llms-full.txt\n-```\n-\n-The pipeline:\n-\n-1. Walks every page in `content/docs/**`.\n-2. For each page, extracts the H1 + frontmatter description for `llms.txt`.\n-3. For each page, includes the full markdown body for `llms-full.txt`.\n-4. Appends the OpenAPI spec from `content/api/authfn.json` (operations + schemas).\n-5. Writes both files into `static/` so they ship with the deployed site.\n+[`llms.txt`](https://llmstxt.org/) is a convention for LLM-friendly project context. This docs site serves two generated files under its existing `/docs` mount:\n+\n+- `https://authfn.com/docs/llms.txt` — short page index.\n+- `https://authfn.com/docs/llms-full.txt` — full documentation context, including the configured API material.\n+\n+These are the configured publication URLs; a local build does not prove live hosting or credentials.\n+\n+## Generation\n+\n+The normal repository build prepares AuthFn's physical dependencies before regenerating OpenAPI and LLM content:\n+\n+```sh\n+npm exec -- turbo run build --filter=@authfn/docs\n+```\n+\n+After those prerequisites are built, run from `authfn/docs`:\n+\n+```sh\n+npm run generate:openapi\n+npm run generate:llms\n+npm run check:llms\n+```\n+\n+`generate:llms` loads `docsfn.config.ts` through the consumer's published DocsFn package and filesystem provider, builds the manifest, and writes `static/llms.txt` and `static/llms-full.txt`. Native prerendered routes publish those files at `/docs/llms.txt` and `/docs/llms-full.txt`; the app-root static copies are not the advertised docs-owner URLs. `check:llms` compares generated content without writing it.\n \n ## Use it\n \n-In Cursor, Claude Desktop, ChatGPT custom GPTs, or anything that accepts a URL as context:\n-\n-```\n-https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/llms-full.txt\n-```\n-\n-For local development against your own fork, you can also point at:\n-\n-```\n-http://localhost:5173/llms.txt\n-http://localhost:5173/llms-full.txt\n-```\n-\n-## What's in `llms-full.txt`\n-\n-Roughly:\n+Supply the full-context URL to an assistant that accepts URLs:\n \n ```text\n-# authfn — Self-hosted authentication for any stack\n-\n-> Sessions, OTP, passwords, social OAuth, 2FA, API keys, multi-region...\n-\n-## Welcome\n-[full body of index.md]\n-\n-## Getting Started\n-[full body of getting-started.md]\n-\n-## Core Concepts › Architecture\n-[full body of architecture.md]\n-\n-...\n-\n-## OpenAPI\n-- POST /sign-up/password — Create a new account with email and password.\n-- POST /sign-in/password — Sign in with email and password.\n-[...all 31 operations...]\n-```\n-\n-## Customizing\n-\n-The `docs:llms` task is configurable via `docsfn.config.ts`:\n-\n-```ts\n-{\n-  // ...\n-  llmsTxt: {\n-    enabled: true,\n-    includeOpenApi: true,\n-    includePages: ['docs/**'],\n-    excludePages: ['docs/blog/**'],\n-  },\n-}\n-```\n-\n-## Token budget\n-\n-| File | Approximate size |\n-| --- | --- |\n-| `llms.txt` | ~8 KB (~2,000 tokens) |\n-| `llms-full.txt` | ~250 KB (~65,000 tokens) |\n-\n-For models with smaller context windows, prefer `llms.txt` and use the [MCP server](./mcp) for on-demand retrieval.\n+https://authfn.com/docs/llms-full.txt\n+```\n+\n+The default local docs port is 6005:\n+\n+```text\n+http://localhost:6005/docs/llms.txt\n+http://localhost:6005/docs/llms-full.txt\n+```\n+\n+## Configuration and size\n+\n+The manifest follows the configured content directories and `site.canonicalUrl` in `docsfn.config.ts`. The shared `scripts/docs-site/llms.mjs` builder passes the canonical URL and excludes Blog posts with the published `includeBlog:false` option. There is no consumer `docs:llms` script or invented `llmsTxt` configuration object.\n+\n+Generated sizes vary with the corpus and API schema. Prefer the short index for smaller context budgets; retrieve individual linked pages or use the separately configured [MCP server](./mcp) when appropriate. Static context files do not authenticate an assistant or establish a live MCP connection.\n \n ---\n \n--- historical-plan/filefn/docs/static/llms.txt\n+++ native-generated212/filefn/docs/static/llms.txt\n@@ -4,105 +4,105 @@\n \n ## Docs\n \n-- [Welcome to filefn](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs): Self-hosted, framework-agnostic file uploads, storage, and processing for TypeScript, Python, and Swift. Resumable uploads, signed URLs, share links, OPFS offline, and a built-in processing pipeline — opt in to what you need.\n-- [Adapters](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters): filefn talks to the outside world through three adapter interfaces — storage, db, and processors. Each one is a small, well-defined contract you can swap.\n-- [DB adapters](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/db): The Adapter contract — what every database backend must implement, and which schemas filefn touches.\n-- [Drizzle DB adapter](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/db-drizzle): createDrizzleAdapter — the Drizzle-ORM-backed adapter for Postgres, SQLite, and MySQL.\n-- [Memory DB adapter](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/db-memory): createMemoryAdapter — an in-process, in-RAM adapter for tests, CI, and ephemeral demos.\n-- [Postgres DB adapter](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/db-postgres): createPostgresAdapter — direct pg-backed adapter for production filefn deployments.\n-- [SQLite DB adapter](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/db-sqlite): createSQLiteAdapter — better-sqlite3 / bun:sqlite for single-instance filefn deployments.\n-- [Processors](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/processors): The bundled processor catalog from @filefn/processing — thumbnails, PDF previews, OCR, image transforms, video, audio, compression.\n-- [Storage adapters](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage): The StorageAdapter contract — what every backend must implement, and how the kernel uses it.\n-- [Azure Blob adapter](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage-azure): createAzureStorage — production-grade storage on Azure Blob with SAS URLs and block blob multipart.\n-- [GCS adapter](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage-gcs): createGCSStorage — production-grade storage on Google Cloud Storage with signed URLs, multipart upload via XML API, and ADC support.\n-- [Local FS adapter](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage-local): createLocalStorage — disk-backed storage for development, demos, and single-instance deployments.\n-- [MinIO adapter](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage-minio): createS3Storage configured for self-hosted MinIO — S3-compatible storage on your own hardware.\n-- [Cloudflare R2 adapter](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage-r2): createS3Storage configured for Cloudflare R2 — egress-free production storage with full S3 multipart compatibility.\n-- [S3 adapter](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage-s3): createS3Storage — production-grade storage on AWS S3 with signed-URL multipart, optional CDN fronting, and SSE.\n-- [AI Resources](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources): Use filefn from your AI coding assistant — llms.txt, MCP server, Skills, and editor configurations.\n-- [Claude](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/claude): Wire filefn into Claude Desktop and Claude Code — MCP server, CLAUDE.md, and recommended hints.\n-- [Codex](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/codex): Wire filefn into OpenAI Codex / GPT-based agents — system prompt bundle, JSON schema, and tool wrappers.\n-- [Cursor](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/cursor): Wire filefn into Cursor — MCP server, project rules, and recommended .cursorrules.\n-- [llms.txt](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/llms-txt): The static AI surfaces — llms.txt, llms-full.txt, llms-rich.json — generated on every docs build.\n-- [MCP server](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/mcp): Connect AI assistants to live filefn docs via the Model Context Protocol — search, fetch, and reference.\n-- [Skills](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/skills): Pre-baked filefn workflows your AI assistant can run on your behalf — install, configure, debug, extend.\n-- [API Reference](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/api): HTTP endpoints exposed by filefn — annotated OpenAPI spec covering uploads, downloads, share links, grants, processing, and capabilities.\n-- [Core Concepts](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts): How filefn is wired — upload sessions, multipart, policies, render intents, the canonical envelope/error model, share links, grants, dedup, offline, events, and security.\n-- [Architecture](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/architecture): How filefn separates upload sessions, files, share links, grants, processing, and adapters into composable subsystems.\n-- [Artifacts](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/artifacts): How filefn models processing output — thumbnails, PDF previews, OCR text, transcoded video, audio waveforms — as first-class file rows.\n-- [Deduplication](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/dedup): Optional content-addressable storage with policy- and tenant-scoped checksums.\n-- [Errors](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/errors): filefn's canonical error envelope and the full error code catalog.\n-- [Events](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/events): The canonical filefn event catalog — upload.started, part.recorded, file:uploaded, processing.*, file:deleted — with redaction rules.\n-- [Grants](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/grants): Per-user / per-tenant permission grants with the four canonical capabilities — read, write, delete, share — and TTLs.\n-- [Idempotency](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/idempotency): How filefn deduplicates upload-init calls and prevents accidental duplicate sessions.\n-- [Multipart](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/multipart): How filefn negotiates chunk size, total parts, and the two upload modes (signed-URL vs. proxy).\n-- [Observability](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/observability): How filefn handles request IDs, structured logging, metrics, and secret redaction.\n-- [Offline](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/offline): How @filefn/client stages uploads in OPFS, replays on reconnect, and serves pending-local previews.\n-- [Policies](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/policies): Named upload policies that gate content type, size, visibility, storage target, and storage path layout.\n-- [Rate limiting](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/rate-limiting): Per-route rate limits using @superfunctions/middleware on top of filefn's upload, download, share-download, and artifact-download endpoints.\n-- [Render intents](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/render-intents): How filefn returns the right artifact for a given UI surface — thumbnail, preview, full, or download — with placeholder fallback.\n-- [Security](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/security): filefn's threat model — what it protects, what it doesn't, and how to harden a deployment.\n\n[Showing lines 1-446 of 1745 (50.0KB limit). Use offset=447 to continue.]"}],"details":{"truncation":{"content":"--- historical-plan/authfn/docs/static/llms.txt\n+++ native-generated212/authfn/docs/static/llms.txt\n@@ -4,116 +4,116 @@\n \n ## Documentation\n \n-- [Custom database adapter](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/database/custom) — Implement the `@superfunctions/db` adapter contract for any storage backend.\n-- [Drizzle adapter](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/database/drizzle) — Use Drizzle ORM with Postgres, MySQL, SQLite, or Cloudflare D1.\n-- [Database adapters](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/database) — Wire authfn to your database — Drizzle, raw Postgres, SQLite, in-memory, or a custom adapter.\n-- [Memory adapter](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/database/memory) — In-memory database — for tests and local development. Don't use it in production.\n-- [Postgres adapter](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/database/postgres) — Use Postgres with authfn through the supported Drizzle adapter path.\n-- [SQLite adapter](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/database/sqlite) — Single-file local database — perfect for local-first apps and self-hosted single-node deployments.\n-- [Adapters](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters) — Database, mail, and OAuth — bring your own. authfn ships only the contracts.\n-- [Custom mail adapter](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/mail/custom) — The delivery contract is one method — drop in any mailer.\n-- [Mail adapters](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/mail) — authfn never bundles a mail provider. Implement the delivery contract for the provider you already use.\n-- [Postmark](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/mail/postmark) — Use Postmark as your authfn email provider.\n-- [Resend](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/mail/resend) — Use Resend as your authfn email provider.\n-- [SendGrid](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/mail/sendgrid) — Use SendGrid as your authfn email provider.\n-- [AWS SES](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/mail/ses) — Use AWS SES as your authfn email provider.\n-- [OAuth adapters](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/oauth) — Bundled OAuth providers and how to extend.\n-- [Admin](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/admin) — @authfn/admin — admin-only routes for listing and deleting users, with a customizable authorization callback.\n-- [Claude](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/claude) — One-click Claude Desktop / Claude Code setup for authfn.\n-- [Codex / Droid](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/codex) — AGENTS.md / Factory Codex / Factory Droid setup for authfn.\n-- [Cursor](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/cursor) — One-click Cursor setup for authfn — MCP, llms.txt, and rules.\n-- [AI Resources](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources) — First-class integrations for LLM-based coding assistants — llms.txt, an MCP server, and per-tool skill packs.\n-- [llms.txt](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/llms-txt) — Static context files generated from the docs — drop into your assistant for one-shot RAG.\n-- [MCP server](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/mcp) — A Model Context Protocol server that exposes authfn docs, OpenAPI, and example apps as live tools for your assistant.\n-- [Skills](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/ai-resources/skills) — Packaged authfn patterns your assistant can apply on request — sign-in flows, OAuth, multi-region, and more.\n-- [API Reference](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/api) — HTTP endpoints exposed by authfn, generated from your enabled plugins.\n-- [Account linking](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/account-linking) — When authfn merges multiple sign-in methods into a single user, and how to control that policy.\n-- [Architecture](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/architecture) — How the authfn kernel, plugins, hooks, runtime resolver, and database adapter fit together.\n-- [Cookies](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/cookies) — Names, prefixes, domain, SameSite, Secure, path, and max-age — every knob authfn exposes for cookie-based sessions.\n-- [CSRF protection](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/csrf) — How authfn defends against cross-site request forgery, what's protected, what's exempt, and how to extend the policy.\n-- [Envelopes](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/envelopes) — Every authfn HTTP response wraps either a success payload or a structured error in a stable envelope shape.\n-- [Errors](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/errors) — Every error code authfn can return — what it means, what HTTP status it maps to, whether it's retryable, and what the client should show.\n-- [Hooks](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/hooks) — Lifecycle callbacks that let you intercept and augment authfn flows — before/after user creation, session issuance, OTP send, OAuth start/callback, and account deletion.\n-- [Core Concepts](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts) — The mental model behind authfn — kernel, plugins, sessions, cookies, runtime, regions, observability, and the canonical envelope/error contract.\n-- [Observability](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/observability) — Every meaningful action in authfn emits a structured event. Wire one callback and you have audit, metrics, and tracing.\n-- [OpenAPI](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/openapi) — How authfn generates and serves an OpenAPI 3.1 document of your enabled plugin set.\n-- [Plugins](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/plugins) — How authfn plugins compose into a single auth runtime — lifecycle, ordering, configuration, schema, and authoring custom plugins.\n-- [Rate limiting](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/rate-limiting) — What authfn does and doesn't enforce, and how to add the limits you need.\n-- [Multi-region routing](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/regions) — Direct regional authorities and canonical-gateway routing for region-pinned AuthFn identity data.\n-- [Runtime resolver](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/runtime) — How authfn decides per-request issuer, base URL, OAuth credentials, and cookie domain — and how to take control of that decision.\n-- [Security](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/security) — authfn's threat model, what it defends against, what it expects you to handle, and how to harden a deployment.\n-- [Sessions](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/sessions) — How authfn issues, verifies, rotates, and revokes sessions — for cookie-based browsers and bearer-token clients alike.\n-- [account-settings](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/account-settings) — Post-login UX — two-factor enroll/disable, API-key list/create/revoke, and protected-endpoint authentication via API key.\n-- [Examples](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples) — Five runnable example apps that exercise authfn end-to-end with deterministic Playwright tests.\n-- [multi-region-routing](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/multi-region-routing) — Two regional authfn servers and a single browser client — region lookup, wrong-authority correction, and successful continuation.\n-- [otp-recovery](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/otp-recovery) — Verify-email OTP, OTP sign-in, password reset via OTP, with a deterministic demo inbox for Playwright assertions.\n-- [password-sessions](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/password-sessions) — Email/password sign-up, sign-in, current-session fetch, multi-device session listing, single-session revoke, and sign-out.\n-- [social-oauth](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/social-oauth) — OAuth start, callback, and disconnect — with a local fake provider so the example doesn't need real third-party credentials.\n-- [Bun](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/bun) — Use Bun's native HTTP server — authfn is WHATWG-native, no adapter needed.\n-- [Bring your own framework](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/byo) — Mount authfn anywhere that hands you a Request — including bare http.createServer, Deno.serve, and AWS Lambda.\n-- [Express](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/express) — Mount authfn on Express with @superfunctions/http-express.\n-- [FastAPI](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/fastapi) — Mount the Python authfn kernel on FastAPI.\n-- [Flask](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/flask) — Mount the Python authfn kernel on Flask.\n-- [Hono](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/hono) — Mount authfn on Hono with @superfunctions/http-hono.\n-- [Frameworks](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks) — Adapters that mount the authfn router on the HTTP framework you already use.\n-- [Next.js (App Router)](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/nextjs) — Mount authfn as a Route Handler and read sessions from RSC, Server Actions, and middleware.\n-- [Starlette](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/starlette) — Starlette apps mount Python authfn through FastAPI — there is no dedicated Starlette extra.\n-- [SvelteKit](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/sveltekit) — Mount authfn as a SvelteKit endpoint and read sessions from server load functions, hooks, and form actions.\n-- [Getting Started](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/getting-started) — Declare an authfn app, create its server runtime, mount it on Hono, and sign in from the typed client.\n-- [Welcome to authfn](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs) — Self-hosted, framework-agnostic authentication for TypeScript and Python. Sessions, OTP, passwords, social OAuth, API keys, 2FA, multi-region — opt in to what you need.\n-- [API keys plugin](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/api-keys) — User-owned and server-issued unowned API keys — hashed, scoped, revocable.\n-- [Authoring custom plugins](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/authoring) — Step-by-step guide to writing your own authfn plugin — schema, routes, hooks, observability, and OpenAPI integration.\n-- [Email OTP plugin](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/email-otp) — One-time codes for email verification, magic-link sign-in, sign-up, and password reset.\n-- [Plugins](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins) — Every authfn capability is a plugin. Pick the ones you need; skip the rest.\n-- [Multi-region plugin](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/multi-region) — Region pinning, lookup, runtime overlays, and wrong-authority redirects.\n-- [Native handoff plugin](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/native-handoff) — Bridge a web sign-in into a native app session — for iOS / macOS / Android wrappers around a web flow.\n-- [Password plugin](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/password) — Email + password sign-up, sign-in, reset via OTP, and optional Have-I-Been-Pwned compromise checks.\n-- [Apple](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/social-oauth/apple) — Configuring Sign in with Apple — JWT-based client secret, the form_post response_mode, and the native iOS / macOS flow.\n-- [Custom OAuth providers](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/social-oauth/custom-providers) — How to add a provider that's not in the bundled set — Microsoft, GitLab, Discord, Slack, your own IdP.\n-- [GitHub](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/social-oauth/github) — Configuring GitHub OAuth — emails endpoint, scopes, organization membership.\n-- [Google](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/social-oauth/google) — Configuring Google OAuth — client setup, scopes, hosted-domain restriction, and one-tap.\n-- [Social OAuth plugin](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/social-oauth) — Google, Apple, and GitHub OAuth — plus a resolver hook for custom providers and a native Apple flow for iOS.\n-- [Two-factor (TOTP) plugin](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/plugins/two-factor) — RFC 6238 TOTP with recovery codes, time-window tolerance, and pluggable encryption.\n-- [Bun](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/bun) — Run authfn under Bun's native HTTP server with zero compatibility shims.\n-- [Quickstart](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart) — Pick the stack you're shipping on and get a working authfn server and client in 10 minutes.\n-- [Next.js (App Router)](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/nextjs) — Mount authfn as a Next.js Route Handler and read sessions from server components and Server Actions.\n-- [Node.js (Hono / Express)](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/nodejs) — Stand up an authfn server on Node with Hono or Express, talk to it from the typed client, and switch from the in-memory adapter to Postgres when you're ready.\n-- [Python (FastAPI / Flask)](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/python) — Run authfn on a Python backend — same routes, same envelopes, same OpenAPI as the TypeScript kernel.\n-- [SvelteKit](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/sveltekit) — Mount authfn as a SvelteKit endpoint, hydrate a reactive session store, and use authfn from server load functions and form actions.\n-- [Swift (iOS / macOS)](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/swift) — Sign into authfn from a SwiftUI app using bearer tokens, native Apple Sign-In, and web ↔ native handoff.\n-- [Account deletion](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/account-deletion) — Cascade-delete a user across every plugin's tables.\n-- [Adding 2FA](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/adding-2fa) — Enroll, confirm, and challenge with TOTP — the full UX.\n-- [Adding a password to an OAuth or OTP account](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/adding-password) — Let an authenticated user opt in to passwords without creating a new account.\n-- [Building an admin tool](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/admin-tool) — Mount @authfn/admin behind your corporate IAM and ship a UI for support.\n-- [Canonical-gateway multi-region](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/canonical-gateway-multi-region) — One stable AuthFn authority, canonical identity placement, signed cell forwarding, migrations, and an operator runbook.\n-- [CLI authentication](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/cli-auth) — Issue an API key from your web UI and use it from a command-line tool.\n-- [Adding a custom OAuth provider (Microsoft Entra)](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/custom-oauth-provider) — Wire authfn to a provider that's not in the bundled set — Microsoft Entra here, but the pattern works for anything.\n-- [Custom validation hooks](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/custom-validation) — Use beforeUserCreate / beforeSessionIssue to enforce organization-specific rules.\n-- [Email verification](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/email-verification) — Verify a user's email after sign-up. Copy-paste recipe.\n-- [Google One Tap](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/google-one-tap) — Hand a Google Identity Services credential to authfn for a frictionless web sign-in.\n-- [Recipes](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes) — Copy-pasteable solutions for common authfn flows. Each recipe is small, self-contained, and links to the relevant plugins / SDKs.\n-- [Magic link sign-in](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/magic-link) — Send a clickable sign-in link instead of asking the user to type a code. Same kernel; different UI.\n-- [Multi-region deployment](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/multi-region-deployment) — Two regions, a globally-replicated lookup store, and a client that routes by email.\n-- [Native mobile handoff](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/native-mobile-handoff) — Sign in via a system browser, hand the session off to your iOS / Android app as a bearer credential.\n-- [OpenTelemetry instrumentation](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/observability-otel) — Wire authfn's observability events into OpenTelemetry spans for full distributed tracing.\n-- [Password reset](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/password-reset) — Full password-reset flow — start, verify, change. Two routes, three UI screens.\n-- [Placement-bound auth context](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/placement-bound-auth-context) — Trusted in-process and private-service AuthFn context for regional application data planes.\n-- [Rotating 2FA encryption keys](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/rotate-2fa-encryption) — What the shipped two-factor plugin encrypts, and how to change keys without a private crypto API.\n-- [Changelog](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/changelog) — Release history of authfn and @authfn/* packages.\n-- [Configuration](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/configuration) — Every option you can pass to authfn() and app.createServer().\n-- [Envelopes](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/envelopes) — The shape of every authfn HTTP response — success and error.\n-- [Error codes](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/errors) — Every AuthFnErrorCode the kernel emits — HTTP status, retryability, when it fires.\n-- [Observability events](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/events) — Every AuthFnEventType the kernel emits — when it fires and what's in its metadata.\n-- [Reference](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference) — The exhaustive reference — every route, error, event, table, envelope, and configuration option.\n-- [HTTP routes](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/routes) — Every authfn route, organized by plugin. Auto-generated from the OpenAPI spec.\n-- [Database schema](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/schema) — Every table generated by `@superfunctions/cli generate-schema` — fields, types, and relationships.\n-- [@authfn/client](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk/client) — The TypeScript client — works in browsers, Node, Bun, Deno; cookie or bearer; with an automatic CSRF, region-aware routing, and a typed surface for every kernel route.\n-- [authfn](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk/core) — The Node server kernel — declare an app with authfn(), then inject runtime dependencies with createServer().\n-- [SDKs](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk) — First-party authfn SDKs — Node kernel, TypeScript client, Svelte bindings, Python kernel, Swift client.\n-- [Python SDK](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk/python) — The authfn Python kernel — same routes, same envelopes, same OpenAPI as the TypeScript kernel, on FastAPI / Flask / Starlette.\n-- [@authfn/svelte](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk/svelte) — Svelte stores, context, and SvelteKit-friendly patterns for displaying sign-in state and reacting to session changes.\n-- [Swift SDK (AuthFnSwift)](https://authfn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk/swift) — The Swift package for iOS / macOS — bearer-token sessions, native Sign in with Apple, web→native handoff, and SwiftUI helpers.\n+- [Custom database adapter](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/database/custom) — Implement the `@superfunctions/db` adapter contract for any storage backend.\n+- [Drizzle adapter](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/database/drizzle) — Use Drizzle ORM with Postgres, MySQL, SQLite, or Cloudflare D1.\n+- [Database adapters](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/database) — Wire authfn to your database — Drizzle, raw Postgres, SQLite, in-memory, or a custom adapter.\n+- [Memory adapter](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/database/memory) — In-memory database — for tests and local development. Don't use it in production.\n+- [Postgres adapter](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/database/postgres) — Use Postgres with authfn through the supported Drizzle adapter path.\n+- [SQLite adapter](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/database/sqlite) — Single-file local database — perfect for local-first apps and self-hosted single-node deployments.\n+- [Adapters](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters) — Database, mail, and OAuth — bring your own. authfn ships only the contracts.\n+- [Custom mail adapter](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/mail/custom) — The delivery contract is one method — drop in any mailer.\n+- [Mail adapters](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/mail) — authfn never bundles a mail provider. Implement the delivery contract for the provider you already use.\n+- [Postmark](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/mail/postmark) — Use Postmark as your authfn email provider.\n+- [Resend](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/mail/resend) — Use Resend as your authfn email provider.\n+- [SendGrid](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/mail/sendgrid) — Use SendGrid as your authfn email provider.\n+- [AWS SES](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/mail/ses) — Use AWS SES as your authfn email provider.\n+- [OAuth adapters](https://authfn.com/docs/adapters/oauth) — Bundled OAuth providers and how to extend.\n+- [Admin](https://authfn.com/docs/admin) — @authfn/admin — admin-only routes for listing and deleting users, with a customizable authorization callback.\n+- [Claude](https://authfn.com/docs/ai-resources/claude) — One-click Claude Desktop / Claude Code setup for authfn.\n+- [Codex / Droid](https://authfn.com/docs/ai-resources/codex) — AGENTS.md / Factory Codex / Factory Droid setup for authfn.\n+- [Cursor](https://authfn.com/docs/ai-resources/cursor) — One-click Cursor setup for authfn — MCP, llms.txt, and rules.\n+- [AI Resources](https://authfn.com/docs/ai-resources) — First-class integrations for LLM-based coding assistants — llms.txt, an MCP server, and per-tool skill packs.\n+- [llms.txt](https://authfn.com/docs/ai-resources/llms-txt) — Static context files generated from the docs — drop into your assistant for one-shot RAG.\n+- [MCP server](https://authfn.com/docs/ai-resources/mcp) — A Model Context Protocol server that exposes authfn docs, OpenAPI, and example apps as live tools for your assistant.\n+- [Skills](https://authfn.com/docs/ai-resources/skills) — Packaged authfn patterns your assistant can apply on request — sign-in flows, OAuth, multi-region, and more.\n+- [API Reference](https://authfn.com/docs/api) — HTTP endpoints exposed by authfn, generated from your enabled plugins.\n+- [Account linking](https://authfn.com/docs/core-concepts/account-linking) — When authfn merges multiple sign-in methods into a single user, and how to control that policy.\n+- [Architecture](https://authfn.com/docs/core-concepts/architecture) — How the authfn kernel, plugins, hooks, runtime resolver, and database adapter fit together.\n+- [Cookies](https://authfn.com/docs/core-concepts/cookies) — Names, prefixes, domain, SameSite, Secure, path, and max-age — every knob authfn exposes for cookie-based sessions.\n+- [CSRF protection](https://authfn.com/docs/core-concepts/csrf) — How authfn defends against cross-site request forgery, what's protected, what's exempt, and how to extend the policy.\n+- [Envelopes](https://authfn.com/docs/core-concepts/envelopes) — Every authfn HTTP response wraps either a success payload or a structured error in a stable envelope shape.\n+- [Errors](https://authfn.com/docs/core-concepts/errors) — Every error code authfn can return — what it means, what HTTP status it maps to, whether it's retryable, and what the client should show.\n+- [Hooks](https://authfn.com/docs/core-concepts/hooks) — Lifecycle callbacks that let you intercept and augment authfn flows — before/after user creation, session issuance, OTP send, OAuth start/callback, and account deletion.\n+- [Core Concepts](https://authfn.com/docs/core-concepts) — The mental model behind authfn — kernel, plugins, sessions, cookies, runtime, regions, observability, and the canonical envelope/error contract.\n+- [Observability](https://authfn.com/docs/core-concepts/observability) — Every meaningful action in authfn emits a structured event. Wire one callback and you have audit, metrics, and tracing.\n+- [OpenAPI](https://authfn.com/docs/core-concepts/openapi) — How authfn generates and serves an OpenAPI 3.1 document of your enabled plugin set.\n+- [Plugins](https://authfn.com/docs/core-concepts/plugins) — How authfn plugins compose into a single auth runtime — lifecycle, ordering, configuration, schema, and authoring custom plugins.\n+- [Rate limiting](https://authfn.com/docs/core-co