{"type":"message","id":"685f6fb8","parentId":"261603c7","timestamp":"2026-10-06T11:25:33.320Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_IpySZ9EO4S1ILIWDaCddkCWZ|fc_055fb762f51dfcfc016ac4daac794087d08f228836edf38baa","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"-- [Share links](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/share-links): Tokenised, optionally auth-required, optionally download-capped public links to files.\n-- [Storage targets](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/storage-targets): Logical storage tiers (durable, temporary) and how to route originals and artifacts to different backends.\n-- [Upload sessions](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/upload-sessions): The lifecycle of a single filefn upload — from init to complete, with every state transition and recovery hook.\n-- [Visibility](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/core-concepts/visibility): How filefn enforces public, private, and shared file access.\n-- [Examples](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples): End-to-end runnable filefn examples — full-stack demo and a production-shaped reference setup.\n-- [Full demo](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/full-demo): Browser uploads, thumbnails, download / delete, smoke tests — Hono + SvelteKit + memory adapter + local FS storage.\n-- [Production setup](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/examples/production): Multi-policy filefn server with auth, processing, S3 storage, and Postgres rows — production-shaped reference.\n-- [Features](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features): Every bundled filefn feature, documented end to end — uploads, downloads, share links, grants, processing, dedup, quotas, offline, HEIC, policies, rate limiting.\n-- [Dedup](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/dedup): Optional content-addressable storage with policy- and tenant-scoped checksums.\n-- [Downloads](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/downloads): How filefn returns signed download URLs, proxies download bytes, and handles version-targeted downloads.\n-- [Grants](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/grants): Per-user / per-tenant permission grants — read, write, delete, share — with TTLs.\n-- [HEIC preprocessing](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/heic): Automatic HEIC → JPEG conversion in @filefn/client and FileFnSwift.\n-- [Offline](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/offline): OPFS-staged offline uploads, automatic resume on reconnect, and pending-local previews.\n-- [Policies](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/policies): Named upload contracts that gate content type, size, visibility, storage target, and storage path layout.\n-- [Processing](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/processing): The filefn processing pipeline — thumbnails, PDF previews, OCR, image transforms, audio waveforms, video posters — with composable processors and stable artifacts.\n-- [Quota](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/quota): Pluggable storage quotas — per-user, per-tenant, or any custom dimension.\n-- [Rate limiting](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/rate-limiting): Per-route rate limiting on upload-init, sign, complete, download, share-download, and artifact-download.\n-- [Share links](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/share-links): Opt-in tokenised, optionally auth-required, optionally download-capped public links to files.\n-- [Uploads](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/uploads): Multipart upload sessions with signed-URL or proxy modes, idempotency, anonymous tokens, and automatic recovery.\n-- [Versions](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/features/versions): Every successful filefn upload becomes a new fileVersions row — list, target, download, and roll back.\n-- [Frameworks](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks): Production wiring for filefn behind every major web framework — Hono, Express, Bun, SvelteKit, Next.js, FastAPI, Flask.\n-- [Bun](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/bun): Native Bun.serve integration — fastest start-up, native multipart streaming, and zero-cold-start production deployments.\n-- [Express](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/express): Production-grade Express integration for filefn — Web Request bridging, body streaming for proxy uploads, CSRF, and rate limiting.\n-- [FastAPI](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/fastapi): Production-grade FastAPI integration — Starlette request bridging, async session resolvers, and reverse-proxy guidance.\n-- [Flask](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/flask): Production-grade Flask integration — WSGI request bridging, async filefn calls via run_async, and reverse-proxy guidance.\n-- [Hono](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/hono): Production-grade Hono integration for filefn — CORS, CSRF for proxy uploads, rate limiting, observability, and graceful 404 fallthrough.\n-- [Next.js](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/nextjs): Production-grade Next.js integration — App Router catch-all route, edge-vs-Node runtime tradeoffs, server actions for upload init.\n-- [SvelteKit](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/frameworks/sveltekit): Production-grade SvelteKit integration — server-side filefn, client-side @filefn/client, hooks-based session forwarding, and RPC-style file APIs.\n-- [Getting Started](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/getting-started): Install filefn, mount it on Hono, configure a storage policy, and upload your first file end to end in 15 minutes.\n-- [Quickstart](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart): Per-framework setup walkthroughs for filefn — Node + Hono, Express, Bun, SvelteKit, Next.js App Router, Python + FastAPI, Python + Flask, and Swift on iOS.\n-- [Bun Quickstart](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/bun): Run @filefn/server on Bun.serve with native Fetch Request / Response.\n-- [Express Quickstart](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/express): Mount @filefn/server on Express by adapting Node's IncomingMessage to a Fetch Request.\n-- [FastAPI Quickstart](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/fastapi): Mount filefn (Python kernel) on a FastAPI app with a single sub-app route.\n-- [Flask Quickstart](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/flask): Mount filefn (Python kernel) as a Flask Blueprint.\n-- [Hono Quickstart](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/hono): Mount @filefn/server on a Hono app on Node, Bun, or Cloudflare Workers in a few lines.\n-- [Next.js Quickstart](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/nextjs): Mount filefn on Next.js App Router with a route.ts catch-all under app/filefn/[...path].\n-- [SvelteKit Quickstart](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/sveltekit): Mount filefn on SvelteKit with a +server.ts catch-all under /filefn.\n-- [Swift Quickstart](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/quickstart/swift): Use FileFnClient on iOS and macOS via Swift Package Manager — foreground and background uploads, HEIC preprocessing, and SwiftUI helpers.\n-- [Recipes](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes): Production-tested filefn patterns — opinionated, end-to-end, copy-and-adjust.\n-- [CDN integration](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/cdn-integration): Serve filefn-managed bytes through CloudFront, Cloud CDN, or Cloudflare without breaking signed URLs or auth.\n-- [Custom processor](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/custom-processor): Author a processor end-to-end — image watermarking, sentiment scoring, transcoding to a custom format — and ship it as an artifact.\n-- [Document uploads](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/document-uploads): PDF / DOCX / plain text uploads with first-page previews, OCR text extraction, and download URLs.\n-- [HEIC conversion](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/heic-conversion): Handle iPhone HEIC photos correctly in browsers, native iOS, and on the server — without any of them seeing HEIC.\n-- [Image uploads](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/image-uploads): Production-grade image upload pipeline with HEIC handling, EXIF stripping, thumbnails, and previews.\n-- [OPFS offline](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/opfs-offline): Reliable uploads when the network is bad — OPFS-staged content with automatic resume on reconnect.\n-- [Signed share links](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/signed-share-links): Common share-link patterns — one-time, time-bound, password-protected, IP-restricted.\n-- [Tenant isolation](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/tenant-isolation): Per-tenant storage paths, quotas, grants, and authorization — the production isolation pattern.\n-- [Video uploads](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/video-uploads): Large-file video uploads with multi-resolution transcoding, poster frames, and queued processing.\n-- [Virus scanning](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/recipes/virus-scanning): Plug ClamAV (or any AV provider) into the filefn processing pipeline as a custom processor.\n-- [Reference](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference): The canonical reference for filefn — every route, every error, every event, every schema, every config knob, the changelog.\n-- [Changelog](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/changelog): Notable filefn releases and migration notes.\n-- [Configuration](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/configuration): Every option in FileFnConfig — types, defaults, and effects.\n-- [Envelopes](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/envelopes): The success and error wrapper every filefn route returns.\n-- [Errors](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/errors): Every filefn error code with its HTTP status, message, common causes, and fixes.\n-- [Events](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/events): Every filefn event with its payload, when it fires, and what's redacted.\n-- [Routes](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/routes): Every HTTP route filefn exposes — methods, paths, request shapes, response shapes, error codes.\n-- [Schema](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/reference/schema): Every filefn DB table — columns, types, indexes — and how to migrate them.\n-- [SDKs](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk): TypeScript server, browser client, Python kernel, native Swift package, viewer resolver, and WKWebView bridge — every filefn SDK speaks the same routes and envelopes.\n-- [@filefn/client](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk/client): The browser / Node / Bun / Deno client SDK — uploadFile, resumeUpload, resolveRenderable, OPFS offline, HEIC preprocessing.\n-- [filefn (Python)](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk/python): The Python kernel — create_file_fn, FileFnConfig, processors, providers.\n-- [@filefn/server](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk/server): The Node / Bun / Workers server kernel — createFileFn, FileProvider, Authorizer, QuotaProvider, and processor authoring.\n-- [Swift](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk/swift): The native Swift Package Manager target — FileFnClient, foreground / background uploaders, SwiftUI helpers, secret-store-backed token recovery.\n-- [Swift bridge](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk/swift-bridge): Native-backed WKWebView bridge — FileFnWebViewBridgeHost on the Swift side, @filefn/swift-bridge on the JS side, with asset-handle uploads and bridge-scheme previews.\n-- [@filefn/viewer](https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/sdk/viewer): Framework-agnostic render-intent resolver utilities for filefn clients.\n+- [Welcome to filefn](https://filefn.com/docs): Self-hosted, framework-agnostic file uploads, storage, and processing for TypeScript, Python, and Swift. Resumable uploads, signed URLs, share links, OPFS offline, and a built-in processing pipeline — opt in to what you need.\n+- [Adapters](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters): filefn talks to the outside world through three adapter interfaces — storage, db, and processors. Each one is a small, well-defined contract you can swap.\n+- [DB adapters](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/db): The Adapter contract — what every database backend must implement, and which schemas filefn touches.\n+- [Drizzle DB adapter](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/db-drizzle): createDrizzleAdapter — the Drizzle-ORM-backed adapter for Postgres, SQLite, and MySQL.\n+- [Memory DB adapter](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/db-memory): createMemoryAdapter — an in-process, in-RAM adapter for tests, CI, and ephemeral demos.\n+- [Postgres DB adapter](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/db-postgres): createPostgresAdapter — direct pg-backed adapter for production filefn deployments.\n+- [SQLite DB adapter](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/db-sqlite): createSQLiteAdapter — better-sqlite3 / bun:sqlite for single-instance filefn deployments.\n+- [Processors](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/processors): The bundled processor catalog from @filefn/processing — thumbnails, PDF previews, OCR, image transforms, video, audio, compression.\n+- [Storage adapters](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage): The StorageAdapter contract — what every backend must implement, and how the kernel uses it.\n+- [Azure Blob adapter](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage-azure): createAzureStorage — production-grade storage on Azure Blob with SAS URLs and block blob multipart.\n+- [GCS adapter](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage-gcs): createGCSStorage — production-grade storage on Google Cloud Storage with signed URLs, multipart upload via XML API, and ADC support.\n+- [Local FS adapter](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage-local): createLocalStorage — disk-backed storage for development, demos, and single-instance deployments.\n+- [MinIO adapter](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage-minio): createS3Storage configured for self-hosted MinIO — S3-compatible storage on your own hardware.\n+- [Cloudflare R2 adapter](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage-r2): createS3Storage configured for Cloudflare R2 — egress-free production storage with full S3 multipart compatibility.\n+- [S3 adapter](https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage-s3): createS3Storage — production-grade storage on AWS S3 with signed-URL multipart, optional CDN fronting, and SSE.\n+- [AI Resources](https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources): Use filefn from your AI coding assistant — llms.txt, MCP server, Skills, and editor configurations.\n+- [Claude](https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources/claude): Wire filefn into Claude Desktop and Claude Code — MCP server, CLAUDE.md, and recommended hints.\n+- [Codex](https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources/codex): Wire filefn into OpenAI Codex / GPT-based agents — system prompt bundle, JSON schema, and tool wrappers.\n+- [Cursor](https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources/cursor): Wire filefn into Cursor — MCP server, project rules, and recommended .cursorrules.\n+- [llms.txt](https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources/llms-txt): The static AI surfaces — llms.txt, llms-full.txt, llms-rich.json — generated on every docs build.\n+- [MCP server](https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources/mcp): Connect AI assistants to live filefn docs via the Model Context Protocol — search, fetch, and reference.\n+- [Skills](https://filefn.com/docs/ai-resources/skills): Pre-baked filefn workflows your AI assistant can run on your behalf — install, configure, debug, extend.\n+- [API Reference](https://filefn.com/docs/api): HTTP endpoints exposed by filefn — annotated OpenAPI spec covering uploads, downloads, share links, grants, processing, and capabilities.\n+- [Core Concepts](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts): How filefn is wired — upload sessions, multipart, policies, render intents, the canonical envelope/error model, share links, grants, dedup, offline, events, and security.\n+- [Architecture](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/architecture): How filefn separates upload sessions, files, share links, grants, processing, and adapters into composable subsystems.\n+- [Artifacts](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/artifacts): How filefn models processing output — thumbnails, PDF previews, OCR text, transcoded video, audio waveforms — as first-class file rows.\n+- [Deduplication](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/dedup): Optional content-addressable storage with policy- and tenant-scoped checksums.\n+- [Errors](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/errors): filefn's canonical error envelope and the full error code catalog.\n+- [Events](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/events): The canonical filefn event catalog — upload.started, part.recorded, file:uploaded, processing.*, file:deleted — with redaction rules.\n+- [Grants](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/grants): Per-user / per-tenant permission grants with the four canonical capabilities — read, write, delete, share — and TTLs.\n+- [Idempotency](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/idempotency): How filefn deduplicates upload-init calls and prevents accidental duplicate sessions.\n+- [Multipart](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/multipart): How filefn negotiates chunk size, total parts, and the two upload modes (signed-URL vs. proxy).\n+- [Observability](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/observability): How filefn handles request IDs, structured logging, metrics, and secret redaction.\n+- [Offline](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/offline): How @filefn/client stages uploads in OPFS, replays on reconnect, and serves pending-local previews.\n+- [Policies](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/policies): Named upload policies that gate content type, size, visibility, storage target, and storage path layout.\n+- [Rate limiting](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/rate-limiting): Per-route rate limits using @superfunctions/middleware on top of filefn's upload, download, share-download, and artifact-download endpoints.\n+- [Render intents](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/render-intents): How filefn returns the right artifact for a given UI surface — thumbnail, preview, full, or download — with placeholder fallback.\n+- [Security](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/security): filefn's threat model — what it protects, what it doesn't, and how to harden a deployment.\n+- [Share links](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/share-links): Tokenised, optionally auth-required, optionally download-capped public links to files.\n+- [Storage targets](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/storage-targets): Logical storage tiers (durable, temporary) and how to route originals and artifacts to different backends.\n+- [Upload sessions](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/upload-sessions): The lifecycle of a single filefn upload — from init to complete, with every state transition and recovery hook.\n+- [Visibility](https://filefn.com/docs/core-concepts/visibility): How filefn enforces public, private, and shared file access.\n+- [Examples](https://filefn.com/docs/examples): End-to-end runnable filefn examples — full-stack demo and a production-shaped reference setup.\n+- [Full demo](https://filefn.com/docs/examples/full-demo): Browser uploads, thumbnails, download / delete, smoke tests — Hono + SvelteKit + memory adapter + local FS storage.\n+- [Production setup](https://filefn.com/docs/examples/production): Multi-policy filefn server with auth, processing, S3 storage, and Postgres rows — production-shaped reference.\n+- [Features](https://filefn.com/docs/features): Every bundled filefn feature, documented end to end — uploads, downloads, share links, grants, processing, dedup, quotas, offline, HEIC, policies, rate limiting.\n+- [Dedup](https://filefn.com/docs/features/dedup): Optional content-addressable storage with policy- and tenant-scoped checksums.\n+- [Downloads](https://filefn.com/docs/features/downloads): How filefn returns signed download URLs, proxies download bytes, and handles version-targeted downloads.\n+- [Grants](https://filefn.com/docs/features/grants): Per-user / per-tenant permission grants — read, write, delete, share — with TTLs.\n+- [HEIC preprocessing](https://filefn.com/docs/features/heic): Automatic HEIC → JPEG conversion in @filefn/client and FileFnSwift.\n+- [Offline](https://filefn.com/docs/features/offline): OPFS-staged offline uploads, automatic resume on reconnect, and pending-local previews.\n+- [Policies](https://filefn.com/docs/features/policies): Named upload contracts that gate content type, size, visibility, storage target, and storage path layout.\n+- [Processing](https://filefn.com/docs/features/processing): The filefn processing pipeline — thumbnails, PDF previews, OCR, image transforms, audio waveforms, video posters — with composable processors and stable artifacts.\n+- [Quota](https://filefn.com/docs/features/quota): Pluggable storage quotas — per-user, per-tenant, or any custom dimension.\n+- [Rate limiting](https://filefn.com/docs/features/rate-limiting): Per-route rate limiting on upload-init, sign, complete, download, share-download, and artifact-download.\n+- [Share links](https://filefn.com/docs/features/share-links): Opt-in tokenised, optionally auth-required, optionally download-capped public links to files.\n+- [Uploads](https://filefn.com/docs/features/uploads): Multipart upload sessions with signed-URL or proxy modes, idempotency, anonymous tokens, and automatic recovery.\n+- [Versions](https://filefn.com/docs/features/versions): Every successful filefn upload becomes a new fileVersions row — list, target, download, and roll back.\n+- [Frameworks](https://filefn.com/docs/frameworks): Production wiring for filefn behind every major web framework — Hono, Express, Bun, SvelteKit, Next.js, FastAPI, Flask.\n+- [Bun](https://filefn.com/docs/frameworks/bun): Native Bun.serve integration — fastest start-up, native multipart streaming, and zero-cold-start production deployments.\n+- [Express](https://filefn.com/docs/frameworks/express): Production-grade Express integration for filefn — Web Request bridging, body streaming for proxy uploads, CSRF, and rate limiting.\n+- [FastAPI](https://filefn.com/docs/frameworks/fastapi): Production-grade FastAPI integration — Starlette request bridging, async session resolvers, and reverse-proxy guidance.\n+- [Flask](https://filefn.com/docs/frameworks/flask): Production-grade Flask integration — WSGI request bridging, async filefn calls via run_async, and reverse-proxy guidance.\n+- [Hono](https://filefn.com/docs/frameworks/hono): Production-grade Hono integration for filefn — CORS, CSRF for proxy uploads, rate limiting, observability, and graceful 404 fallthrough.\n+- [Next.js](https://filefn.com/docs/frameworks/nextjs): Production-grade Next.js integration — App Router catch-all route, edge-vs-Node runtime tradeoffs, server actions for upload init.\n+- [SvelteKit](https://filefn.com/docs/frameworks/sveltekit): Production-grade SvelteKit integration — server-side filefn, client-side @filefn/client, hooks-based session forwarding, and RPC-style file APIs.\n+- [Getting Started](https://filefn.com/docs/getting-started): Install filefn, mount it on Hono, configure a storage policy, and upload your first file end to end in 15 minutes.\n+- [Quickstart](https://filefn.com/docs/quickstart): Per-framework setup walkthroughs for filefn — Node + Hono, Express, Bun, SvelteKit, Next.js App Router, Python + FastAPI, Python + Flask, and Swift on iOS.\n+- [Bun Quickstart](https://filefn.com/docs/quickstart/bun): Run @filefn/server on Bun.serve with native Fetch Request / Response.\n+- [Express Quickstart](https://filefn.com/docs/quickstart/express): Mount @filefn/server on Express by adapting Node's IncomingMessage to a Fetch Request.\n+- [FastAPI Quickstart](https://filefn.com/docs/quickstart/fastapi): Mount filefn (Python kernel) on a FastAPI app with a single sub-app route.\n+- [Flask Quickstart](https://filefn.com/docs/quickstart/flask): Mount filefn (Python kernel) as a Flask Blueprint.\n+- [Hono Quickstart](https://filefn.com/docs/quickstart/hono): Mount @filefn/server on a Hono app on Node, Bun, or Cloudflare Workers in a few lines.\n+- [Next.js Quickstart](https://filefn.com/docs/quickstart/nextjs): Mount filefn on Next.js App Router with a route.ts catch-all under app/filefn/[...path].\n+- [SvelteKit Quickstart](https://filefn.com/docs/quickstart/sveltekit): Mount filefn on SvelteKit with a +server.ts catch-all under /filefn.\n+- [Swift Quickstart](https://filefn.com/docs/quickstart/swift): Use FileFnClient on iOS and macOS via Swift Package Manager — foreground and background uploads, HEIC preprocessing, and SwiftUI helpers.\n+- [Recipes](https://filefn.com/docs/recipes): Production-tested filefn patterns — opinionated, end-to-end, copy-and-adjust.\n+- [CDN integration](https://filefn.com/docs/recipes/cdn-integration): Serve filefn-managed bytes through CloudFront, Cloud CDN, or Cloudflare without breaking signed URLs or auth.\n+- [Custom processor](https://filefn.com/docs/recipes/custom-processor): Author a processor end-to-end — image watermarking, sentiment scoring, transcoding to a custom format — and ship it as an artifact.\n+- [Document uploads](https://filefn.com/docs/recipes/document-uploads): PDF / DOCX / plain text uploads with first-page previews, OCR text extraction, and download URLs.\n+- [HEIC conversion](https://filefn.com/docs/recipes/heic-conversion): Handle iPhone HEIC photos correctly in browsers, native iOS, and on the server — without any of them seeing HEIC.\n+- [Image uploads](https://filefn.com/docs/recipes/image-uploads): Production-grade image upload pipeline with HEIC handling, EXIF stripping, thumbnails, and previews.\n+- [OPFS offline](https://filefn.com/docs/recipes/opfs-offline): Reliable uploads when the network is bad — OPFS-staged content with automatic resume on reconnect.\n+- [Signed share links](https://filefn.com/docs/recipes/signed-share-links): Common share-link patterns — one-time, time-bound, password-protected, IP-restricted.\n+- [Tenant isolation](https://filefn.com/docs/recipes/tenant-isolation): Per-tenant storage paths, quotas, grants, and authorization — the production isolation pattern.\n+- [Video uploads](https://filefn.com/docs/recipes/video-uploads): Large-file video uploads with multi-resolution transcoding, poster frames, and queued processing.\n+- [Virus scanning](https://filefn.com/docs/recipes/virus-scanning): Plug ClamAV (or any AV provider) into the filefn processing pipeline as a custom processor.\n+- [Reference](https://filefn.com/docs/reference): The canonical reference for filefn — every route, every error, every event, every schema, every config knob, the changelog.\n+- [Changelog](https://filefn.com/docs/reference/changelog): Notable filefn releases and migration notes.\n+- [Configuration](https://filefn.com/docs/reference/configuration): Every option in FileFnConfig — types, defaults, and effects.\n+- [Envelopes](https://filefn.com/docs/reference/envelopes): The success and error wrapper every filefn route returns.\n+- [Errors](https://filefn.com/docs/reference/errors): Every filefn error code with its HTTP status, message, common causes, and fixes.\n+- [Events](https://filefn.com/docs/reference/events): Every filefn event with its payload, when it fires, and what's redacted.\n+- [Routes](https://filefn.com/docs/reference/routes): Every HTTP route filefn exposes — methods, paths, request shapes, response shapes, error codes.\n+- [Schema](https://filefn.com/docs/reference/schema): Every filefn DB table — columns, types, indexes — and how to migrate them.\n+- [SDKs](https://filefn.com/docs/sdk): TypeScript server, browser client, Python kernel, native Swift package, viewer resolver, and WKWebView bridge — every filefn SDK speaks the same routes and envelopes.\n+- [@filefn/client](https://filefn.com/docs/sdk/client): The browser / Node / Bun / Deno client SDK — uploadFile, resumeUpload, resolveRenderable, OPFS offline, HEIC preprocessing.\n+- [filefn (Python)](https://filefn.com/docs/sdk/python): The Python kernel — create_file_fn, FileFnConfig, processors, providers.\n+- [@filefn/server](https://filefn.com/docs/sdk/server): The Node / Bun / Workers server kernel — createFileFn, FileProvider, Authorizer, QuotaProvider, and processor authoring.\n+- [Swift](https://filefn.com/docs/sdk/swift): The native Swift Package Manager target — FileFnClient, foreground / background uploaders, SwiftUI helpers, secret-store-backed token recovery.\n+- [Swift bridge](https://filefn.com/docs/sdk/swift-bridge): Native-backed WKWebView bridge — FileFnWebViewBridgeHost on the Swift side, @filefn/swift-bridge on the JS side, with asset-handle uploads and bridge-scheme previews.\n+- [@filefn/viewer](https://filefn.com/docs/sdk/viewer): Framework-agnostic render-intent resolver utilities for filefn clients.\n--- historical-plan/filefn/docs/static/llms-full.txt\n+++ native-generated212/filefn/docs/static/llms-full.txt\n@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@\n \n # Welcome to filefn\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs\n \n \n Self-hosted, framework-agnostic file uploads, storage, and processing for TypeScript, Python, and Swift. Resumable uploads, signed URLs, share links, OPFS offline, and a built-in processing pipeline — opt in to what you need.\n@@ -91,7 +91,7 @@\n \n # Adapters\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters\n \n \n filefn talks to the outside world through three adapter interfaces — storage, db, and processors. Each one is a small, well-defined contract you can swap.\n@@ -135,7 +135,7 @@\n \n # DB adapters\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/db\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/db\n \n \n The Adapter contract — what every database backend must implement, and which schemas filefn touches.\n@@ -209,7 +209,7 @@\n \n # Drizzle DB adapter\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/db-drizzle\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/db-drizzle\n \n \n createDrizzleAdapter — the Drizzle-ORM-backed adapter for Postgres, SQLite, and MySQL.\n@@ -264,7 +264,7 @@\n \n # Memory DB adapter\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/db-memory\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/db-memory\n \n \n createMemoryAdapter — an in-process, in-RAM adapter for tests, CI, and ephemeral demos.\n@@ -306,7 +306,7 @@\n \n # Postgres DB adapter\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/db-postgres\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/db-postgres\n \n \n createPostgresAdapter — direct pg-backed adapter for production filefn deployments.\n@@ -373,7 +373,7 @@\n \n # SQLite DB adapter\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/db-sqlite\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/db-sqlite\n \n \n createSQLiteAdapter — better-sqlite3 / bun:sqlite for single-instance filefn deployments.\n@@ -438,7 +438,7 @@\n \n # Processors\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/processors\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/processors\n \n \n The bundled processor catalog from @filefn/processing — thumbnails, PDF previews, OCR, image transforms, video, audio, compression.\n@@ -636,7 +636,7 @@\n \n # Storage adapters\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage\n \n \n The StorageAdapter contract — what every backend must implement, and how the kernel uses it.\n@@ -707,7 +707,7 @@\n \n # Azure Blob adapter\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage-azure\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage-azure\n \n \n createAzureStorage — production-grade storage on Azure Blob with SAS URLs and block blob multipart.\n@@ -760,7 +760,7 @@\n \n # GCS adapter\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage-gcs\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage-gcs\n \n \n createGCSStorage — production-grade storage on Google Cloud Storage with signed URLs, multipart upload via XML API, and ADC support.\n@@ -825,7 +825,7 @@\n \n # Local FS adapter\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage-local\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage-local\n \n \n createLocalStorage — disk-backed storage for development, demos, and single-instance deployments.\n@@ -872,7 +872,7 @@\n \n # MinIO adapter\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage-minio\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage-minio\n \n \n createS3Storage configured for self-hosted MinIO — S3-compatible storage on your own hardware.\n@@ -944,7 +944,7 @@\n \n # Cloudflare R2 adapter\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage-r2\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage-r2\n \n \n createS3Storage configured for Cloudflare R2 — egress-free production storage with full S3 multipart compatibility.\n@@ -1002,7 +1002,7 @@\n \n # S3 adapter\n \n-Source: https://filefn.superfunctions.dev/docs/adapters/storage-s3\n+Source: https://filefn.com/docs/adapters/storage-s3\n\n[999 more lines in file. Use offset=747 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791285933320}}
